{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like confetti over ancient temples. The air smelled of incense and rain-wet stone. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, listening to the rustle of bamboo groves, and evenings wandering Gion’s lantern-lit alleys, hoping to catch a glimpse of a geisha’s fleeting silhouette.\n\nTokyo was a different rhythm—electric, fast, humming. In Shibuya, I stood in the famous scramble crossing, swallowed by a tide of people, yet somehow feeling utterly alone in the crowd. I devoured steaming ramen at a tiny stall in Shinjuku, where the chef nodded without speaking and refilled my bowl with a smile. Akihabara dazzled with neon and anime, but I found my favorite moment in a quiet bookstore in Jimbocho, flipping through vintage manga under soft reading lamps.\n\nIn Hiroshima, the Peace Memorial Park hummed with stillness. I placed a paper crane beside the Atomic Bomb Dome, its shadow stretching long in the afternoon sun. The silence there taught me more than any museum exhibit.\n\nI rode the Shinkansen to Nara, where deer bowed politely for snacks, and temples stood centuries older than my own country. At night, I stayed in a ryokan, sleeping on tatami mats, wrapped in silk yukata, listening to the drip of a garden fountain.\n\nJapan didn’t just show me beauty—it showed me presence. No one rushed me. No one asked for a photo. They simply lived, beautifully, quietly. I left with a heart fuller than my suitcase.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan.\n\nI remembered how to breathe.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.45900171995162964}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.13951724767684937}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.08629857003688812}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.3484904170036316}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002161430